## 🎯 Common Mistakes When Using **System.Text.Json** (C#)  
Below is a “cheat‑sheet” of the most frequent pitfalls you’ll bump into when you serialize/deserialize JSON with the *built‑in* System.Text.Json library, plus concrete examples and “how‑to” fixes.

| # | Mistake | Why it breaks | How to avoid it | Example |
|---|---------|---------------|-----------------|---------|
| 1 | **Case‑mismatch** – property names in JSON don’t match the C# property names. | By default JSON is *case‑sensitive* and property names are serialized exactly as they appear on the C# type. | 1. Set `PropertyNameCaseInsensitive = true` on `JsonSerializerOptions` for deserialization. <br>2. Use `[JsonPropertyName]` or `[JsonPropertyOrder]` to map. | ```csharp\nclass Person{public string FirstName{get;set;}}\nvar json = \"{\\\"firstName\\\":\\\"Bob\\\"}\";\nvar opt = new JsonSerializerOptions{PropertyNameCaseInsensitive=true};\nvar p = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Person>(json,opt); // works\n``` |
| 2 | **Ignoring non‑public properties** – trying to serialize fields or non‑public setters. | System.Text.Json only serializes *public* properties with public getters/setters (unless explicitly told otherwise). | Use `[JsonInclude]` for read‑only properties or fields, or set `IncludeFields = true`. | ```csharp\nclass Secret{[JsonInclude]private string code{get;set;}=\"XYZ\";}\n``` |
| 3 | **Unsupported types** – e.g., `Tuple`, `KeyValuePair`, `DateOnly`, `TimeOnly`, `BigInteger`. | No built‑in converter for these types. | Implement a custom `JsonConverter<T>` or transform the data to a supported shape. | ```csharp\nclass Wrapper{public Tuple<int,string> Pair{get;set;}}\n// Custom converter for Tuple<int,string>\n``` |
| 4 | **Polymorphic deserialization** – base‑type variable but JSON contains a derived type. | System.Text.Json doesn’t infer the concrete type. | 1. Use `JsonPolymorphic` and `JsonDerivedType` attributes (.NET 7). <br>2. Register a custom `JsonConverter` that reads a discriminator property. | ```csharp\n[JsonPolymorphic]\n[JsonDerivedType(typeof(Cat), \"cat\")]\n[JsonDerivedType(typeof(Dog), \"dog\")]\nabstract class Animal{…}\n``` |
| 5 | **Reference loops** – e.g., `Parent.Child.Parent…`. | System.Text.Json throws `JsonException: Self‑reference loop detected`. | Enable reference handling (`ReferenceHandler.Preserve`) or ignore cycles with a custom converter. | ```csharp\nvar opt = new JsonSerializerOptions{ReferenceHandler = ReferenceHandler.Preserve};``` |
| 6 | **Wrong attribute namespace** – using `Newtonsoft.Json` attributes instead of `System.Text.Json` ones. | The library ignores attributes that belong to a different JSON library. | Stick to `[JsonPropertyName]`, `[JsonIgnore]`, `[JsonConverter]`, etc., in the `System.Text.Json.Serialization` namespace. | ```csharp\n[JsonPropertyName(\"id\")]\npublic int Id{get;set;}\n``` |
| 7 | **Enum serialization as numbers** – expecting string values. | By default enums serialize as integers. | Register `new JsonStringEnumConverter()` in `JsonSerializerOptions`. | ```csharp\nvar opt = new JsonSerializerOptions{Converters = { new JsonStringEnumConverter() }};\n``` |
| 8 | **Null value handling** – nulls are serialized by default, or default values are omitted unexpectedly. | `DefaultIgnoreCondition` defaults to `Never`. | Set `DefaultIgnoreCondition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull` or `WhenWritingDefault`. | ```csharp\nvar opt = new JsonSerializerOptions{DefaultIgnoreCondition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull};\n``` |
| 9 | **Missing `[JsonConstructor]`** – type has only a parameterized constructor. | System.Text.Json can’t instantiate the type. | Decorate the constructor with `[JsonConstructor]` or provide a public parameterless constructor. | ```csharp\nclass Point{[JsonConstructor]public Point(int x,int y){…}}\n``` |
| 10 | **Using `JsonExtensionData` incorrectly** – trying to read unknown properties into a non‑`Dictionary<string, JsonElement>` field. | The property must be `Dictionary<string, JsonElement>` (or `JsonNode` in .NET 6+). | Use the correct type and mark with `[JsonExtensionData]`. | ```csharp\n[JsonExtensionData]\npublic IDictionary<string, JsonElement> Extra{get;set;} = new Dictionary<string,JsonElement>();\n``` |
| 11 | **Thread‑safety of `JsonSerializerOptions`** – mutating a shared options instance. | `JsonSerializerOptions` is *not* thread‑safe for mutation. | Reuse a **read‑only** static options instance; create new instances when you need different settings. | ```csharp\nprivate static readonly JsonSerializerOptions DefaultOptions = new JsonSerializerOptions{WriteIndented=true};\n``` |
| 12 | **Large payloads and memory churn** – using `JsonSerializer.Serialize<T>(obj)` on huge graphs. | Serializing to a string allocates a massive `string`. | Use `Utf8JsonWriter` or `JsonSerializer.SerializeAsync` to stream to `Stream`. | ```csharp\nusing var stream = new MemoryStream();\nawait JsonSerializer.SerializeAsync(stream, bigObj, opt);\n``` |
| 13 | **Deserializing into `Dictionary<string, object>`** – you get `JsonElement` values. | Direct conversion yields `JsonElement`, which is not a .NET CLR type. | Convert `JsonElement` to the target type manually (`element.GetInt32()`, `element.GetString()`, etc.) or use `JsonNode` (`System.Text.Json.Nodes`). | ```csharp\nvar dict = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Dictionary<string, JsonElement>>(json);\nint count = dict[\"count\"].GetInt32();\n``` |
| 14 | **Not setting `PropertyNamingPolicy`** – expecting camelCase when your C# names are PascalCase. | Default is `null` → property names are written as‑is. | Set `PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase`. | ```csharp\nvar opt = new JsonSerializerOptions{PropertyNamingPolicy=JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase};\n``` |
| 15 | **Using `JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingDefault`** on value types that are zero. | The default value of numeric types (0) is considered “default” and will be omitted. | Be careful – if you need to preserve zeros, set `WhenWritingNull` or `Never`. | ```csharp\nopt.DefaultIgnoreCondition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingDefault;\n``` |

