## 📌 Best‑Practice Checklist

| Category | Recommendation | Rationale |
|----------|----------------|-----------|
| **Options** | Create *immutable* `JsonSerializerOptions` per use‑case and reuse them. | Avoid accidental mutation; options are thread‑safe read‑only. |
| **Naming** | Prefer `JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase` if your API uses camelCase; otherwise map each property explicitly with `[JsonPropertyName]`. | Reduces boilerplate and keeps your C# types free of serialization concerns. |
| **Nulls / Defaults** | Decide upfront whether you want to preserve nulls or omit defaults. Use `DefaultIgnoreCondition`. | Keeps JSON payload size small and clear. |
| **Polymorphism** | If you have inheritance, use the built‑in polymorphism support (.NET 7) or a custom converter. | Avoids manual “discriminator” code scattered across the project. |
| **Reference Loops** | If your domain graph contains cycles, enable `ReferenceHandler.Preserve` or design the DTOs to be cycle‑free. | Prevents `JsonException`. |
| **Converters** | Write a single `JsonConverter<T>` for each non‑standard type; register it once in options. | Keeps code DRY and avoids per‑serialization overhead. |
| **Streaming** | For large or high‑frequency payloads, use `Utf8JsonWriter` or `JsonSerializer.SerializeAsync`. | Reduces GC pressure and memory footprint. |
| **Deserialization safety** | Use `[JsonConstructor]` when your type only has a parameterized constructor. | Guarantees proper object creation. |
| **Testing** | Write round‑trip unit tests (`Serialize → Deserialize → Assert`) for each DTO. | Catches hidden mismatches early. |
| **Attribute hygiene** | Keep the *System.Text.Json* attributes in the *System.Text.Json.Serialization* namespace. | Avoids silent no‑ops from mistaken attribute usage. |

---

## 🔍 Quick “Do’s & Don’ts” (One‑liners)

| Do | Don’t |
|----|-------|
| Use `[JsonPropertyName]` for non‑Pascal names. | Use `[JsonProperty]` (Newtonsoft) – it’s ignored. |
| Set `PropertyNameCaseInsensitive = true` for lenient deserialization. | Forget to set it; case mismatches will throw. |
| Register `JsonStringEnumConverter` if you want string enums. | Rely on the default integer output. |
| Mark `JsonExtensionData` property as `Dictionary<string, JsonElement>`. | Use a different collection type. |
| When handling cycles, set `ReferenceHandler.Preserve` or redesign. | Let the serializer blow up on a loop. |
| For DTOs with only readonly properties, add `[JsonInclude]`. | Expect it to serialize automatically. |
| When writing large data, use `Utf8JsonWriter`. | Convert to string first. |
| Create a static readonly options instance for shared configuration. | Rebuild options for every call. |
| Use `[JsonConstructor]` if no public parameterless ctor exists. | Rely on the serializer to magically instantiate. |
| Keep DTOs free of business logic; use separate models. | Mix persistence & domain logic, causing hidden serialization side‑effects. |

